TX_UNIT'S TWO OUTPUT BUFFERS IN C's SHAPE: the port allocated a `Vec` pair per
tx-type TRIAL where C writes into a slot it allocated once per encoder thread
at init. 24.17 M allocator calls per 512x512 photo frame at preset 2 ->
15.14 M (9,296x C -> 5,824x), -956 M Ir (-1.97 %), and the FIRST allocation
removal in this campaign to convert to wall clock: **1.026x faster on a
textured photo at 512 p2**, 1.018x / 1.012x on the gradient at 256 / 512 p2,
byte-identical on both ISAs, and the memory axis unmoved on both quantities
and both hosts. Four variants were measured and rejected on the way; they are
in section 6 of the TSV and below, because two of them cost aarch64 inter peak
RSS for the same arithmetic.

date:   2026-09-05
hosts:  MEASUREMENT r7900x (x86_64-linux, AMD Ryzen 9 7900X, 24 cores),
        valgrind-3.26.0 --tool=callgrind (Ir only), rustc 1.98.1, gcc 15.2.0.
        GATES both: r7900x and this Mac (aarch64-darwin, M4 Pro, bash 5 via
        /opt/homebrew/bin/bash — every gate that uses `mapfile`/`declare -A`
        refuses bash 3.2).
base:   main@origin 41e5d8f1, built in a sibling directory on each host with
        the same command and measured FIRST. It reproduces
        `compute_stats_cshape_2026-09-04`'s AFTER row (p2 1.777, p6 2.349) and
        `callcount_realimg_2026-09-04`'s allocator counts (24,169,163 against
        that record's 24,169,160 — three calls of harness difference) to the
        unit, which is the positive control that the two trees are comparable.
