=================== C's LIFETIME MODEL (cited) ===================

`svt_aom_mode_decision_context_ctor` (`Source/Lib/Codec/md_process.c:214`,
reached from `svt_aom_enc_dec_context_ctor`'s `EB_NEW` at
`enc_dec_process.c:108`) allocates the WHOLE mode-decision scratch ONCE per
encoder thread and reuses it for every block of every frame. The three pools
this chunk is about:

    md_process.c:585-596  svt_aom_pic_buf_desc_pool_ctor(&ctx->tx_recon_coeff_pool, ..., TX_TYPES)
                          svt_aom_pic_buf_desc_pool_ctor(&ctx->tx_recon_pool,       ..., TX_TYPES)
                          svt_aom_pic_buf_desc_pool_ctor(&ctx->tx_quant_coeff_pool, ..., TX_TYPES)
    md_process.c:597-601  ctx->recon_coeff_ptr[txt_itr] = &ctx->tx_recon_coeff_pool.descs[txt_itr];
                          ctx->recon_ptr[txt_itr]       = &ctx->tx_recon_pool.descs[txt_itr];
                          ctx->quant_coeff_ptr[txt_itr] = &ctx->tx_quant_coeff_pool.descs[txt_itr];

released once in the matching dtor (`mode_decision_context_dctor`,
`md_process.c:35`, installed at `:231`; `svt_aom_pic_buf_desc_pool_dctor` at
`:142-144`). The CONSUMER is the tx-type search itself:

    product_coding_loop.c:4723-4725
        EbPictureBufferDesc* recon_ptr       = (tx_type == DCT_DCT) ? cand_bf->recon : ctx->recon_ptr[tx_type];
        EbPictureBufferDesc* quant_coeff_ptr = (tx_type == DCT_DCT) ? cand_bf->quant : ctx->quant_coeff_ptr[tx_type];
    product_coding_loop.c:4949 / :5011      best_tx_type = tx_type;     // an INDEX; nothing is copied
    product_coding_loop.c:5082-5084         if (best_tx_type != DCT_DCT) copy_txt_data(cand_bf, ctx, ...);

So C's per-trial buffer cost is a POINTER SELECT, "keep the winner" is an index
assignment, and the ONE copy per transform unit (`copy_txt_data`, `:4256-4283`)
runs after the loop and only when the winner is not DCT_DCT. BOTH sides of C's
select are themselves pooled — `cand_bf` comes out of `cand_pred_pool` /
`cand_rec_coeff_pool` / `cand_quant_pool` (`md_process.c:640-655`, `max_nics_uv`
slots, also once per thread) — which is why C's whole-encode allocator traffic
is ~2,600 calls at every preset and on every content: it is one-time setup, not
per-block work.

=================== WHAT THE PORT DID, AND WHAT IT DOES NOW ===================

`tx_unit_inner` built and RETURNED `TxUnitOut { qcoeff: Vec<i32>, recon:
Vec<u8>, .. }` — `vec![0i32; pw * ph]` plus `vec![0u8; w * h]` per call, i.e.
per (candidate x tx-type x transform unit) TRIAL. `callcount_realimg_2026-09-04`
ranked it the port's #1 allocator caller: 6,598,459 `calloc`s at photo_cid 512
p2, with the matching frees appearing as `mds3::eval_candidate` 6,224,725 (the
inlined drop of the losing trials).

Now `tx_unit_screened_into(..., out: &mut TxOutBufs)` writes into caller-owned
buffers and returns a `TxUnitMeta` carrying the scalars plus each buffer's
VALID PREFIX LENGTH — explicit, because a pooled buffer keeps the largest
capacity it has ever been grown to, so its `len()` is not this unit's extent,
and `recon_len == 0` is how the old empty-when-the-inverse-is-skipped `Vec`
contract survives. `txt_search` holds two `TxOutBufs` in a per-thread `TXT_OUT`
(C keeps `TX_TYPES` of them because it indexes by `txt_itr`; the port only ever
needs the CURRENT trial and the BEST so far) and a new best does
`mem::swap(cur, bst)` — C's `best_tx_type = tx_type`. The winner is
materialised into an owned `Vec` ONCE per transform unit, where C runs
`copy_txt_data`: measured `txt_search::{closure#0}` = 992,510 mallocs, and
2 x C's `perform_tx_partitioning` count on the same cell (496,519) is 993,038.

WHERE THE PORT DRAWS C's SELECT, AND WHY NOT WHERE C DRAWS IT. C splits on
`tx_type == DCT_DCT` and pays nothing either way (both sides are pooled); the
port's owned output is a REAL allocation, so it splits on `only_dct` — does
this search have more than one candidate — instead. A one-candidate search
(M9+, any dim > 32, and the whole video/inter arm at preset 13, where `txt_on`
is false) has no loser to save an allocation on, so it keeps the pre-existing
owned path byte for byte and allocation for allocation. That is not tidiness:
pooling those searches too is variant 1 below, and it cost +5.8 % aarch64 inter
peak RSS at 2048 for zero instruction saving.

NEITHER POOLED BUFFER IS RE-ZEROED, and that is the point of the change
(`700357e2`'s finding from the other direction: a recycled buffer that is
re-zeroed pays a `memset` that `vec![0; n]`'s `calloc` gets for free). It is
sound because every position is written before it is read on every path:
  * `qcoeff` — `quant_coding::{fp_one, b_one}` (`quant_coding.rs:100-107` /
    `:388-394`) assign `qcoeff[rc]` on BOTH arms of their `if tmp32 != 0`, for
    every `rc` in `0..min(coeffs.len(), qcoeff.len(), dqcoeff.len())`, and the
    slice handed in is exactly `pw * ph` (as is `packed`); the two QM paths open
    with their own `qcoeff[..coeffs.len()].fill(0)` (`qm.rs:134`, `:195`).
    `optimize_b` and `perform_noise_normalization` only revise positions the
    quantizer already wrote.
  * `recon` — `residual::recon_add_clamp` (`residual.rs:277`) writes every one
    of `h * w`; the `eob == 0` arm copies the prediction over every row; the
    coded-lossless arm zips all 16. When the inverse transform is skipped the
    buffer is not touched at all and `recon_len == 0`.

=================== WHY THIS IS NOT ATTEMPTS 1-3 ===================

Three allocation removals are on the record as NULL or NEGATIVE, and this is a
different object from each:

  1. `alloc_bufpool_null_2026-08-13` — a thread-local Vec FREE-LIST. It hands
     out and takes back whole `Vec`s, so there is still a `Vec` per use plus
     pool bookkeeping, and a recycled `Vec` must be re-zeroed to reproduce
     `vec![0; n]`: it removes the allocation and KEEPS the memset, which is the
     half this campaign has measured to be worth nothing. Here the buffer never
     leaves the pool during the search and is never re-zeroed.
  2. The `extract_neighbors_tiled` hoist (2026-09-03) moved an allocation UP a
     level — still one buffer per block, still zeroed. 18-20 % of all
     allocations removed, null. It changed WHERE, not WHETHER.
  3. `mds3d0_null` / `hadscratch_null` (2026-09-03, 0.983-0.992x) each removed
     ONE small allocation per candidate / per call, 0.95 M calls at the top of
     that pair. This removes 9.03 M calls (37 % of the frame's total) and the
     6.6 M zero-fills with them, and it is the only one of the four that changes
     the buffer's LIFETIME to C's.

The rule those three produced — "on this allocator a malloc/free pair is worth
about nothing and a memset is worth real time; rank by what it MEMSETs" — is
not contradicted, it is the design input: 6.6 M `calloc`s are 6.6 M zero-fills.

